WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.040
We're surrounded
by statistical claims,

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:06.920
whether it's a striking graph
going viral on social media,

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.240
a politician claiming that life
has gotten 62% better

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.160
while they've been in office,

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.800
or just something everyday,
like a piece of economic data,

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.240
the numbers are everywhere.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:26.400
So what should we think about them?

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:27.840
Here's one approach -

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.480
don't trust any of them.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:31.920
Mutter something about

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.440
lies, damned lies and statistics.

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:38.720
Or joke that 98% of statistics
are made up on the spot.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:40.720
That seems smart.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:44.040
After all, nobody likes
to be taken for a fool.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:48.640
But we can't just reject
all statistics equally.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:51.200
Statistics can show us
things about the world

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.760
that we can't perceive
in any other way.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:56.440
Instead, we have to exert
a bit of brain power

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.080
to figure out what is true
and what is not.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:05.040
That sounds hard, but perhaps
it's not as hard as you might think.

00:01:05.120 --> 00:01:07.720
So here are three easy rules

00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:09.800
to make statistics add up -

00:01:09.880 --> 00:01:13.080
the three Cs of data wisdom.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:15.600
First, be calm.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.040
Most statistics come packaged up
with emotional baggage.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.360
They're supposed to make us
angry or joyful or afraid.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.200
Staggering deficits,
shocking rates of crime,

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:31.320
inspiring sums
raised for good causes.

00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:35.680
These emotions are why numbers
go viral on social media,

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.880
the reason they end up
in the headlines.

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:40.680
There's nothing wrong
with feeling emotions,

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:42.960
but they don't help us think clearly.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.240
So before you share a claim
that has you hot under the collar,

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:50.520
take a moment to notice
your instinctive reaction -

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:54.200
from rage, to denial, to vindication.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:01:58.680
Once you've noticed it,
look at the statistic again.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:00.800
It may seem different now.

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:03.680
Second, get context.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.200
For example, when the
UK Health Secretary said in 2020

00:02:08.280 --> 00:02:12.520
that the NHS would save
£100 million over five years

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:16.120
if everyone who was overweight
lost a few pounds.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:18.720
What should we make of that claim?

00:02:18.800 --> 00:02:21.960
What does he mean by overweight,
for example?

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:25.240
And what evidence does he have
to support this claim?

00:02:25.960 --> 00:02:27.600
But the most important
piece of context

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.000
is simply to understand whether
£100 million is a big number.

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:34.040
It sounds big.

00:02:34.120 --> 00:02:37.080
But there are 67 million people
in the UK,

00:02:37.160 --> 00:02:40.760
so £100 million is just £1.50 each.

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.400
You can figure that out
on your phone.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:45.840
Now remember that £1.50 each

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:48.120
was spread across five years.

00:02:48.200 --> 00:02:49.840
So that's 30 pence

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:52.320
per person, per year.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:53.720
The Health Secretary then said

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.600
that if every overweight person
lost some weight,

00:02:56.680 --> 00:03:01.920
the NHS would save the equivalent
of 30 pence per UK resident per year.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.440
Not a lot.

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.720
Statistics can be a very
complex subject,

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.400
but you can get a long way with
simple questions about context.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:12.120
What's being measured here?

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:14.360
Is it going up or going down?

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.080
Is it big or small?

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:18.320
What's the source of the claim?

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.280
You don't need a lot of fancy maths,
just a search engine,

00:03:22.360 --> 00:03:25.560
the back of an envelope,
and a curious mind.

00:03:25.640 --> 00:03:30.760
Which brings me to the third
important principle - be curious.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.800
When we use a number as
a weapon in an argument,

00:03:33.880 --> 00:03:36.520
or a prop for our preconceptions,

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:37.920
we learn nothing.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.520
Instead we should think
about statistics

00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.360
as a tool
to understand the world,

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.680
like a telescope for an astronomer.

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:48.600
Ask yourself what a statistical claim

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:50.800
is really telling you
about the world

00:03:50.880 --> 00:03:53.560
and what questions it inspires.

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.280
Of course we don't have time to
do all this for every claim we see,

00:03:58.360 --> 00:04:01.920
so a final habit is to ask yourself
whether the source

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:05.920
of a statistical claim is respecting
the three Cs.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.840
A good journalist will help you be
calm, will give you context,

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.000
and will feed your curiosity.

00:04:12.240 --> 00:04:16.480
A viral 'gotcha' circulating
on Twitter or WhatsApp

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:18.120
will often do the opposite.

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.800
We shouldn't just accept
statistics unthinkingly,

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:25.160
but neither should we dismiss
them without thinking either.

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:31.000
Three simple habits - be calm,
get context, and be curious -

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:33.360
can help the world add up.

